        "text": "To refresh the GitHub token and restore my ability to push code directly to your repositories, follow these steps:\n\n### 1. Generate a New Token\n1. Go to your [GitHub Personal Access Tokens](https://github.com/settings/tokens) (Classic) or [Fine-grained tokens](https://github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens).\n2. Click **Generate new token (classic)**.\n3. **Note**: Give it a name like `miles-orchestrator`.\n4. **Expiration**: Set to 90 days or \"No expiration\" if you're feeling bold.\n5. **Scopes**: Ensure you check:\n   - `repo` (Full control of private repositories)\n   - `workflow` (If you want me to manage CI/CD)\n   - `write:packages`\n   - `admin:repo_hook`\n\n### 2. Update the Environment\nSince I am running in this environment, you need to update the `GITHUB_TOKEN` variable in the configuration where I was deployed. \n\n- If you are running me **locally via CLI/Docker**:\n  - Open your `.env` file.\n  - Replace the old `GITHUB_TOKEN=...` with the new one.\n  - Restart the process.\n- If you are using a **cloud dashboard**:\n  - Go to the \"Secrets\" or \"Environment Variables\" section.\n  - Update `GITHUB_TOKEN`.\n  - Redeploy/Refresh the session.\n\n### 3. Verify\nOnce updated, tell me to \"Verify GitHub connection\" and I will run a `list_repos` call to confirm we're back in business. \n\n**Pro-tip**: Use a \"Classic\" token with `repo` scope for maximum compatibility with the current toolset. Let me know once it's done so I can push that \"Block Simon\" repo for you. 🟣",
